There was a meeting of violinist-conductors Saturday night at Roy Thomson Hall, although Itzhak Perlman is still more violinist than conductor, and Peter Oundjian has long been more conductor than violinist. Still, the TSO music director (whose playing career was ended by a neurological condition in his left hand) managed to pull off his first public performance on the violin in 17 years by joining Perlman in Bach��s Concerto in D Minor for Two Violins.
The orchestra assembled around the soloists was small, so there was no need to push to be heard. Oundjian got a few intonation slips out of his system in the first movement; the Largo (in which the second violin enters first) flowed sweetly and well. There were no serious issues in the finale. Sometimes you had to listen hard to hear who was who, which surely reflects well on Oundjian.
A few jumps in volume by the orchestra in this movement supplied relief to what was otherwise a relatively horizontal performance. There were two conductors on stage, busy with their violins. I wonder whether a third, armed with a baton, would have added an extra dimension.
