For the record, it's important to note that "Baby Driver" is not a sequel to last spring's animated kiddie toon "Boss Baby" -- or of any other movie about talking infants, for that matter. Anyone who loads the kids into the Family Truckster and heads to the theater under that assumption stands to be disappointed.
But they will probably be the only ones.
That's because while it's got far too much gunplay and tough talk to be a kiddie toon, "Baby Driver," the latest film from "Shaun of the Dead" and "Scott Pilgrim vs. the World" director Edgar Wright, is a high-octane, high-style and highly entertaining thrill ride. A subtly innovative blend of cars, guns, music and old-school cool, it's also one of those increasingly rare creatures in Hollywood: an undeniably original movie.
That doesn't just mean it's not a sequel, either. You've seen other heist films before -- and maybe even heist films that dabble in the same basic theme, about a noble soul trying desperately to break from his or her life of crime -- but when seen through Wright's distinct lens, it all feels fresh and new.
While "Baby Driver" boasts a wide range of assets, from the great cast to the fast-moving script, its core is built around its jukebox of classic rock and R&B tunes. From Martha and the Vandellas' "Nowhere to Run" to Focus' "Hocus Pocus" and from Queen's "Brighton Rock" to, yes, Simon and Garfunkel's "Baby Driver," the songs don't just flit around the edges of the film, punctuating and underscoring the action. They become the gas that makes this movie machine go, thematically matching the story while the drums beat in time with the gun blasts and cars' tires wail along with the lyrics.
Without them, "Baby Driver" would be a pretty good movie. With them, it is darn near a great one.
Ansel Elgort ("The Fault in Our Stars") plays the lead role, a whiskerless kid who goes by the nickname "Baby" but who also happens to be a peerless getaway car driver. His employer: a criminal mastermind named Doc (Kevin Spacey, calm, cool and menacing as ever), whose specialty is devising foolproof plans, then hiring just the right team to execute those plans.
Among his rotating cast of psychos are characters played by the likes of Jamie Foxx, Jon Hamm, Jon Bernthal and Flea.
Doc's number one rule: He never uses the same team twice. His number two rule: He always uses Baby to drive the getaway vehicle. Baby's not your typical bad guy, though. As we learn, he doesn't drive because he wants to. He does it because he has to.
Another thing he has to do is listen to music. Turns out he's got a killer case of tinnitus, the result of a car accident when he was a child. The only thing that drowns out the ringing in his ears is constant tuneage.
I have no idea if that's how tinnitus actually works but it seems plausible, at least when Spacey says it. Anyway, the upshot is that to Baby, music isn't just a luxury. It's a necessity. To him, it's like air. When his tunes are playing and his wheels are turning, he can't be caught, simple as that.
Taken as a whole, from the concept to the car stunts, the thing isn't any more or less ridiculous than most of those on display in your garden-variety "Fast & Furious" movie. The difference is that in a stark illustration of the power of good writing and strong directing, they feel believable here. What's more, despite the intricacy of the whole exercise, Wright makes it look both effortless and seamless.
If there's a weak moment, it comes early on -- in the opening credits, in fact -- as Baby is walking down the street, strutting from his gang's clubhouse to a nearby coffee shop. In his ears, Bob & Earl's "Dancing in the Streets" is playing and as he walks, he is indeed dancing in the streets.
The problem is that Elgort doesn't quite have the cool factor to pull off that scene. Wright enlivens things by making it feel like one continuous shot, as Elgort/Baby sidesteps other pedestrians, spins around lightpoles and bounces to the beat, but something about it rings false.
Fortunately, that's pretty much the only glaringly false moment in "Baby Driver." Once the story gets going and Baby starts laying tire tracks through the streets, everything suddenly because buyable. By the time Baby decides he wants to escape from his life of crime -- and, what's more, that he wants to help a pretty young diner waitress (Lily James) escape from hers, too -- it's impossible not to be all in, going along for the ride.
And it is an enjoyable ride -- one of the most enjoyable of the summer so far, in fact.
It's the sort of movie that makes a part of you root for a sequel, because the characters are just so great -- but makes most of you root against a sequel, because the story feels so complete, so right, as it is.
