CLEVELAND, Ohio -- The new Horseshoe Casino Cleveland won't open to the public until 9:30 Monday night, but police will be directing traffic during the morning rush hour.
The debut of Ohio's first casino isn't like a sporting event or the St. Patrick's Day parade, with ticket sales or history serving as a gauge of what to expect.
Eager casino-goers can't begin lining up until 5 p.m. -- moved back from 11 a.m. by Horseshoe officials to accommodate concerns expressed by the city and their landlord, Forest City Enterprises. And 1,500 wristband wearers, winners of a contest on Facebook, will be first to enter.
But Traffic Commissioner James Muhic said he can't discount the possibility that crowds will mass in advance, as they have in other states.
"We don't know what people are going to do," Muhic said.
Muhic has assigned police to 14 intersections along East Ninth and Ontario streets, downtown's two main north-south arteries, to guard against gridlock.
The deployment is less than half of that used for a typical Browns football game. Some posts will be staffed only during the morning and evening rush hours, but officers will be at the majority of the locations from 6:30 a.m. to 1 a.m.
Two cars will patrol the Inner Belt to keep accidents or stalled vehicles from blocking traffic and causing backups on entrance ramps.
Mounted police will maintain order on Public Square while other police search for illegally parked cars and call in tow trucks. Casino officials think many patrons may choose to travel on public buses and trains that go directly to Tower City and Public Square, the site of the casino.
The Ohio Department of Transportation will alleviate one headache by suspending a repaving project at Ontario Street and Carnegie Avenue on Saturday. Work will resume in July.
The traffic officers, marshaled from other parts of the city, will remain downtown for at least six days, Muhic said, though he might adjust hours and stations. How things go during that period will determine what comes next, he said.
Horseshoe officials estimate the casino will draw 5 million visitors a year, or an average of 13,000 a day, but the flow is likely to vary and will be spread over a 24-hour-day schedule. Once the routine is set, casino General Manager Marcus Glover said, crowds might total 6,000 a day early in the week and triple on the weekends.
Caesars Entertainment, which will manage the casino, has promoted the opening beyond the Cleveland area, but company officials have declined to forecast Monday's turnout.
